  <body>
    <sec id="s1">
      <title>Introduction</title>
      <p>Interfacial kinetics govern the rate capability of layered oxide cathodes <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B1">1</xref>. The exchange current density j<sub>0</sub> obeys the Butler–Volmer equation <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B2">2</xref>, and for small overpotentials η the linearized form applies.</p>
      <p>
        <disp-formula id="e1">
          <label>1</label>
          <alternatives>
            <tex-math>j = j_0 \left[ \exp\!\left( \frac{\alpha F \eta}{RT} \right) - \exp\!\left( -\frac{(1-\alpha) F \eta}{RT} \right) \right]</tex-math>
            <mml:math><mml:mrow><mml:mi>j</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>j</mml:mi><mml:mn>0</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math>
          </alternatives>
        </disp-formula>
      </p>
      <p>The temperature dependence of the rate constant is Arrhenius-like:
        <disp-formula id="e2">
          <label>2</label>
          <mml:math>
            <mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>=</mml:mo>
            <mml:mi>A</mml:mi>
            <mml:msup><mml:mi>e</mml:mi>
              <mml:mfrac>
                <mml:mrow><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>E</mml:mi><mml:mi>a</mml:mi></mml:msub></mml:mrow>
                <mml:mrow><mml:mi>R</mml:mi><mml:mi>T</mml:mi></mml:mrow>
              </mml:mfrac>
            </mml:msup>
          </mml:math>
        </disp-formula>
      </p>
    </sec>
    <sec id="s2">
      <title>Experimental</title>
      <sec id="s2a">
        <title>Cell assembly</title>
        <p>Coin cells used a Na metal anode and the working composite described elsewhere <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B3">3,4</xref>. The cell geometry is shown in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Figure 1</xref>.</p>
        <fig id="F1">
          <label>Figure 1</label>
          <caption><p>Schematic of the model cell assembly used for EIS measurements.</p></caption>
          <graphic xlink:href="graphic.png"/>
        </fig>
      </sec>
